☸️ Kubernetes (K8s) - Complete Revision Notes
1. KUBERNETES
Kubernetes (K8s) is an open-source container orchestration platform used to automate the deployment, scaling, and management of containerized applications. It groups containers that make up an application into logical units for easy management and discovery.

🎯 Why Kubernetes?
To manage containers at scale.

To automate deployment, scaling, and operations.

To ensure high availability and reliability.

To optimize resource utilization.

To provide portability across environments (cloud, on-prem, hybrid).

⚡ Key Features
Feature	Description
1. Automation	Automates deployment, scaling, and management.
2. Scalability	Easily scale apps up or down based on demand.
3. Self-Healing	Automatically restarts, replaces or reschedules failed containers.
4. High Availability	Ensures minimal downtime with replication and failover.
5. Load Balancing	Distributes traffic across containers.
6. Service Discovery	Automatically discovers services and endpoints.
7. Rolling Updates & Rollbacks	Update apps without downtime and rollback easily if needed.
8. Portability	Run applications anywhere: on-prem, cloud or hybrid.
💡 Common Use Cases & Benefits
Use Cases: Deploy/manage microservices, run stateless/stateful apps, handle traffic spikes with auto-scaling, manage multi-environment deployments.

Benefits: Faster/reliable deployments, better resource utilization, improved availability, consistent environments, supports hybrid infra.

2. KUBERNETES ARCHITECTURE
A Kubernetes cluster consists of a Control Plane (Master Node) and Worker Nodes working together to run containerized applications.

Think: Control Plane thinks, Worker Nodes do the work.

🧠 Control Plane Components
API Server: Exposes the K8s API. Central point of communication.

etcd: Consistent and highly available key-value store.

Scheduler: Assigns Pods to nodes based on resources & rules.

Controller Manager: Runs controllers to maintain the desired state.

Cloud Controller Manager: Integrates with cloud providers to manage cloud resources.

⚙️ Worker Node Components
Kubelet: Ensures containers are running in a Pod.

Kube-proxy: Manages network rules and service communication.

Container Runtime: Runs containers (e.g., containerd, Docker).

🏢 Cluster Overview
Control Plane (Master Node) manages the cluster.

Worker Nodes run the workloads (pods).

3. Core Components
These components are the main building blocks that make Kubernetes work.

API Server: Frontend for the Kubernetes control plane. Exposes the Kubernetes API and is the central point of all communication.

etcd: Consistent and highly available key-value store. Stores all cluster data and configuration in a reliable way.

Scheduler: Watches for newly created Pods with no node assigned. Selects the best node for the Pod to run on based on resources and policies.

Controller Manager: Runs controllers to maintain the desired state of the cluster. (e.g., Node Controller, Replication Controller, Endpoint Controller).

Kubelet: Runs on each worker node. Ensures containers are running in a Pod. Communicates with the API Server.

Kube Proxy: Runs on each node. Maintains network rules. Enables network communication to Pods from inside and outside the cluster.

⭐ Together, these components ensure the cluster is healthy, scalable, and self-healing.

4. Pods, ReplicaSet, Deployment & Rolling Updates
📦 Pods
The smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes. Represents one or more containers that share the same network, storage, and configuration. Pods are ephemeral.

Pods get their own IP and communicate with other Pods / Services.

🔄 ReplicaSet
Ensures that a specified number of Pod replicas are running at any given time. If a Pod fails, ReplicaSet creates a new Pod to maintain the desired count.

🚀 Deployment
Manages ReplicaSets and provides declarative updates for Pods and ReplicaSets. Supports scaling, rolling updates, and rollbacks.

Deployment manages ReplicaSets and ensures desired state of the application.

⏪ Rolling Update & Rollback
Rolling Update: Updates Pods gradually with zero downtime. New Pods are created first, old Pods are terminated later.

Rollback: Rolls back the Deployment to a previous version if something goes wrong. Restores the old ReplicaSet and Pods.

Key Points:

Pod → smallest unit

ReplicaSet → maintains desired pods

Deployment → manages ReplicaSets, updates, scaling

Rolling Update → zero downtime updates

Rollback → revert to previous stable version

5. SERVICES
A Service in Kubernetes is a stable network endpoint that exposes a set of Pods as a network service. It provides load balancing, service discovery, and a stable IP/DNS name.

Service Type	Description
ClusterIP (Default)	Exposes the Service on a cluster-internal IP. Accessible only within the cluster.
NodePort	Exposes the Service on a static port on each Node's IP. Accessible externally via <NodeIP>:<NodePort> (Port range: 30000-32767).
LoadBalancer	Exposes the Service externally using a cloud provider's load balancer. Auto-provisions a load balancer and gets a stable external IP.
Ingress	Provides HTTP/HTTPS routing to Services based on host names or paths. Operates at Layer 7. Needs an Ingress Controller (e.g., NGINX).
6. NAMESPACES, LABELS, SELECTORS, CONFIGMAP & SECRET
Namespaces: Provide a way to divide cluster resources between multiple users or projects. Helps in organizing and isolating resources. Default namespaces: default, kube-system, kube-public, kube-node-lease.

Labels: Key-value pairs attached to Kubernetes objects (e.g., app=frontend, env=prod). Used to organize and identify objects easily.

Selectors: Used to filter resources based on labels. Used by Services, ReplicaSets, and Deployments to find matching Pods. (Equality-based or Set-based).

📝 ConfigMap & Secret
ConfigMap: Stores non-confidential configuration data in key-value pairs. Can be used as environment variables, command-line arguments, or mounted files.

Secret: Stores sensitive data like passwords, tokens, keys, etc. Data is stored in base64 encoded format (not encrypted by default).

YAML Example (ConfigMap):

yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: app-config
data:
  APP_NAME: my-app
  LOG_LEVEL: info
7. Volumes, PV, PVC & StorageClass
Kubernetes Volumes provide persistent storage for containers. PV, PVC, and StorageClass work together to make storage dynamic, scalable, and manageable.

Volumes: Directories accessible to containers in a Pod. Types: emptyDir, hostPath, configMap, secret, persistentVolume.

Persistent Volume (PV): A piece of storage in the cluster provisioned by an admin or dynamically using StorageClass. It is a cluster resource, independent of any Pod.

Persistent Volume Claim (PVC): A request for storage by a user. Binds to a suitable PV that matches its requirements.

StorageClass: Provides a way to dynamically provision storage. Defines the type of storage (EBS, EFS, etc.) and helps in automatic provisioning of PV.

8. StatefulSet, DaemonSet, Job & CronJob
StatefulSet: Manages stateful applications. Provides stable, unique network identities and persistent storage. Pods are created in order and deleted in reverse order (e.g., Databases like MySQL, MongoDB).

DaemonSet: Ensures that a copy of a Pod runs on all (or selected) Nodes. Useful for node-level tasks like logging, monitoring, networking.

Job: Creates Pods that run to completion and then stop. Suitable for one-time tasks like batch processing or data migration.

CronJob: Runs Jobs on a schedule (like cron). Useful for recurring tasks like backups, reports, cleanup jobs.

📊 Quick Comparison
Workload	Purpose	Key Points
StatefulSet	Stateful applications	Stable identity, ordered, persistent storage
DaemonSet	Run on all nodes	One Pod per node
Job	Run once to completion	Terminates after success/failure
CronJob	Run on a schedule	Creates Jobs based on cron schedule
9. SCHEDULING
Scheduling is the process of assigning Pods to Nodes based on resource requirements, policies, and constraints.

Node Selector: Assigns Pods to Nodes with specific labels (spec.nodeSelector: disktype: ssd). Simple way to control placement. Uses exact match.

Affinity: More advanced rules for scheduling.

nodeAffinity (on nodes)

podAffinity (with other Pods)

podAntiAffinity (avoid Pods)

Supports soft (preferred) & hard (required) rules.

Taints: Applied to Nodes to repel Pods. Pods without matching toleration will not be scheduled. Helps reserve Nodes for specific workloads.

Tolerations: Applied to Pods to allow them to be scheduled on tainted Nodes. Must match the taint key, value, and effect.

Taints & Tolerations Together: Taints repel Pods. Tolerations allow Pods. Used together to control where Pods can run.

📊 Quick Comparison
Concept	Purpose	Applies To
Node Selector	Simple label-based selection	Pod
Affinity	Advanced placement rules	Pod
Taints	Repel Pods from Nodes	Node
Tolerations	Allow Pods on tainted Nodes	Pod
💡 Think: Taints control which Pods can run on a Node. Tolerations allow Pods to run on tainted Nodes.

10. SECURITY
Kubernetes provides multiple security mechanisms to protect your cluster, applications, and data.

RBAC (Role-Based Access Control): Controls who can perform actions on Kubernetes resources. Uses Roles (namespaced) and ClusterRoles (cluster scoped). Bind them to users, groups, or service accounts. Follows the principle of least privilege.

Service Account: Provides an identity for processes running in Pods. Used by Pods to interact with the Kubernetes API. Each namespace has a default Service Account.

Network Policy: Controls the traffic flow to and from Pods. Defines allowed/denied rules based on Pods, namespaces, IP blocks, and ports. Provides isolation between applications.

🛡️ Security Goals
Authentication: Who are you?

Authorization: What can you do?

Isolation: Keep workloads separated.

Audit & Monitoring: Track & detect issues.

Best Practices: Use RBAC (don't use cluster-admin), create least-privilege ServiceAccounts, restrict network traffic with NetworkPolicies, keep monitoring & auditing enabled.

